  "lineage": "African-American declamatory poetry performed over jazz, funk, and hand percussion, the direct proto-hip-hop lineage of The Last Poets (formed 1968 in Harlem) and Gil Scott-Heron with Brian Jackson (e.g. The Revolution Will Not Be Televised, 1970). Politically charged, rhythmically delivered spoken verse rides congas and jazz-funk grooves, growing out of the Black Arts Movement and Harlem's earlier Beat and jazz-poetry readings. The delivery foregrounds cadence, repetition, and rhetorical build over sung melody, and it is widely regarded as a foundational ancestor of rap. Recordings were often cut in loft and small studio settings in New York.",
